# component-man.pkg -- one component's manual pages, and the two gaps that
# become visible the moment the manual is cut per component.
#
# Measured against the machine, system-wide, the manual is wrong in both
# directions: shipped programs with no page at all, and pages for programs we do
# not install (`man vi', the dos* family).  System-wide, that is one number
# nobody owns.  Per component it is somebody's: net's 23 programs have 0 pages,
# mgr-clients' 50 have 2, base's 173 have 152.
#
# THE CORPUS IS THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN.  hostbuild/extract-man.sh assembles
# hostbuild/build/man out of the pages every component tracks in its own tree --
# base/man, net/man, tools/man, editors/man and the rest -- so a manual package
# is cut from material this repository holds and versions, and there is no
# third-party archive to fetch before one can be.
#
# The package is THE PAGES FOR WHAT THIS COMPONENT BUILDS: the rows of man.index
# whose name column is a program the -bin half installs, and the articles for the
# LIBRARIES its list's `libraries' line names.  A library's manual belongs with
# the component that builds it for the same reason a program's does: net's
# twenty-six socket pages, from accept() to socket(), are the manual for
# something net ships, against one page for its twenty-three programs, and a
# rule about programs alone leaves them in no package at all.  Which pages
# document a library is read from the pages, where the Lexicon already says it:
# a title line is `accept() -- Sockets Function (libsocket)'.
#
# A component's own man/ directory is not the unit, because those directories are
# not one-to-one with components -- base/man alone holds 666 of the 1,206 pages,
# which is the whole reference manual, and packing it whole would put `man vi'
# into a package for an image that has no vi.  Two consequences, both wanted:
#
#   A COMPONENT WITH NO PAGES CANNOT CUT ONE.  `component.py component <n> man'
#   refuses rather than writing an empty archive, because an empty archive that
#   unpacked and passed a gate is read as coverage.  Nine components are in that
#   position and the libraries do not move any of them: not one of the nine
#   builds a library the Lexicon describes.
#   AN ARTICLE NO COMPONENT CLAIMS IS NEVER PACKED.  It stays in the assembled
#   manual, which is where the whole reference lives and where an image's
#   /usr/man is staged from, and it is not offered as though it documented a
#   program this system installs.
#
# man.index is GENERATED here, holding exactly the rows for the pages present.
# /bin/man scans it a line at a time and cannot reach a page that has no row, so
# an index copied wholesale would promise 1,206 articles to a tree holding 152.
kind      component-man
what      the manual pages for one component's programs and libraries, and an index of exactly those
producer  dist/pack-component.sh
archive   c900-@C@-man-v@V@.tar.gz
toplevel  c900-@C@-man-v@V@
version   VERSION
stamp     .provenance  kind commit version package component pkgkind entries programs documented libraries contentid
text      .provenance  ^package=@C@-man$
text      .provenance  ^pkgkind=man$
text      .provenance  ^component=@C@$
# What this package does NOT cover, said in the package rather than in a report
# somebody has to go and find: `programs' is the component's executable count and
# `documented' is how many of them have a page here.  They are allowed to differ
# -- that is the state of the manual -- but the difference is carried with the
# artifact, so nobody reads a man package as the component being documented.
# Both count PROGRAMS: a library's articles are pages in the archive and are not
# added to `documented', which would otherwise report net as documenting
# twenty-seven of twenty-three programs.  `libraries' names whose articles the
# rest of the pages are -- `libsocket' for net, `-' for a component that builds
# no library the Lexicon describes -- so the count and the archive agree without
# anybody opening it.
file      LICENSE
# ---- THE TERMS THE CONTENTS ARE UNDER ----
# LICENSE above is what this repository says about its OWN files.  What the
# component's CONTENTS are under travels with the release, not inside each
# archive: the texts are collected once into `licences' beside the packages,
# with an index.tab saying which regimes govern which component.  A source
# package carries each program's own COPYING beside the code as well, because
# it ships the source root upstream put it in.
file      manifest.tab
dir       files
file      files/man.index
# THE SECTION IS NOT ALWAYS 1.  net-man and mgr-man carry nothing but COHERENT.2
# pages -- screen(2) and the two MGR articles -- so a declaration naming
# COHERENT.1 would fail for them while passing for every component whose pages
# happen to be in section 1.  What a man package must carry is a page in SOME
# section and an index row for it, which is what these say.
glob      files/COHERENT.*/*  1
text      manifest.tab  ^f COHERENT[.]
text      files/man.index  ^COHERENT[.]
# ---- WHICH BYTES ----
# nroff source, so a content id is recomputable and is recorded.  The pages are
# the manual's text as this repository tracks it; what this package adds is the
# SELECTION, and .contents is what makes the selection checkable.
file      .contents
file      check-contents.sh
idfile    .contents  .provenance  contentid
gate      check-contents.sh
